{"qid":"Q849623","names":["Oyo Empire"],"title":"Oyo Empire","endonym":null,"capsule":"The Oyo Empire was a Yoruba state in what is now southwestern Nigeria, founded by the legendary Oranmiyan and reorganizing as a major cavalry power after a period of exile in the late 16th century. Under the Alafin (king) and the powerful Oyo Mesi council, Oyo dominated the savanna and forest-fringe trade routes, exporting enslaved people to European merchants at the coast and controlling the Dahomey tributary state from 1730. At its 18th-century apogee Oyo was the largest territorial power in West Africa's interior. The empire collapsed rapidly in the early 19th century under internal political crises, Fulani jihad pressure from Ilorin (which seceded c. 1817), and the disruption of old trade patterns; the capital Old Oyo was sacked and abandoned around 1835–36.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Core Oyo territory is reasonably attested; peripheral tributary zones and the maximal extent of suzerainty in different periods are less precisely defined, as Oyo power was exercised through tribute networks rather than direct administration.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Oyo Empire","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oyo_Empire"},{"label":"Britannica: Oyo empire","url":"https://www.britannica.com/topic/Oyo-empire"},{"label":"Britannica: Yoruba — The Oyo kingdom","url":"https://www.britannica.com/topic/Yoruba"}],"britannica":"place/Oyo-empire","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Oyo Empire","rulers":[{"qid":null,"name":"Kanran","nativeName":"Kanran","roman":"Kanran","start":1659,"end":1665,"image":null,"imageUrl":nul